The original [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says “one issue, one fix queued.”
The exported data and the logic behind its own table disagree: the shared date parser and
the booking form's date hint are separate problems requiring separate tickets and resolutions.
The analysis below describes the export; the later desk update supplies their current disposition.

## Desk update: two different resolutions

The [course challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update)
now shows an engineering update: “#4471 · closed 05 oct”. Read on 30 October 2026:

- **Parser — FIXED:** “fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales”. Close the parser issue as completed.
- **Hint — WON'T FIX:** “booking-v3 form retired”. Close the form-hint issue as not planned;
  retiring the form is not a fix to its hint.

These resolutions come from the desk's update, not from changes to production code in this repository.
The export predates that update. Its historical counts and the five anomalous reports remain intact;
the update does not supply individual reproductions for those anomalies. The proposed checks below
record the original analysis, not tests we have run against production.

## Count the reports, then classify them

<ReviewEvidence section="counts" />

<div class="table-scroll"><CauseTable ticket={4471} /></div>

The table above uses the supplied `uniqueById`, `groupByCause`, `causeOf`, and tenant directory.
Its `#4471` marker identifies the existing ticket's majority cause; “no ticket of its own”
describes the exported desk system, not the new GitHub issues prepared for this review.

## Parser: the tenant writes the day first; the shared parser reads the month first

The shared parser reads month first regardless of the tenant's date convention.
For a day-first tenant, an input meaning 7 April can become 4 July.
The affected group includes app and email reports, and form reports using the older booking form.
Changing the visible hint alone does not repair this parsing path.

The parser ticket should make parsing follow an explicit, agreed date format and preserve
the intended calendar date. It should test both ambiguous dates (where both numbers are valid months)
and unambiguous dates, across the affected input channels. These are proposed acceptance checks;
the export does not contain the production parser to patch or reproduce directly.

## Form hint: what people are told to type disagrees with how the form reads it

For day-first tenants using `booking-v3`, form submissions are a separate `hint` group.
The form displays `MM/DD/YYYY`, but reads the entry according to the tenant's date order.
A person following that hint can therefore get a different date despite following the instructions.
Fix the hint and the form's interpretation together; do not assume a parser fix elsewhere fixes it.

The form ticket should check the displayed instruction, the submitted value, and the resulting
calendar date together for each affected tenant. App or email reports from those same tenants
still belong to the parser group: tenant identity alone is not sufficient to classify the report.

## Receipt dates show impact; merge dates show desk work

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

<div class="table-scroll"><ReviewEvidence section="timeline" /></div>

Reports start arriving before the large merge batch. The batch is not evidence that the bug began
on the merge date, and a falling merge count is not evidence that users stopped experiencing it.
The export continues to contain received reports after that batch. Use received time to describe
reported impact, and merge time to describe the desk's handling of the queue.

## Keep the unusual reports visible

<Callout kind="warn">
  A deterministic classification is not a verified root cause for every individual report.
  Preserve the desk's counts while retaining the contrary evidence.
</Callout>

<ReviewEvidence section="exceptions" />

These descriptions include one-day shifts, different displayed days across cities, and an invite
arriving before booking. They do not establish that month/day swapping explains every symptom.
The supplied `readAs` function deliberately annotates these reports inside their existing group.
Follow that counting convention, retain the exact report IDs and descriptions, and ask the desk to
reproduce the anomalous cases with the original input, stored date, timezone, and delivery timestamps.
Timezone conversion or delivery ordering are hypotheses to investigate, not diagnoses established here.
If investigation confirms another cause, reassign the affected IDs and recompute the totals rather
than double-counting them.

## Triage by cause, not by the subject line

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) and
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) tell the desk to merge matching subjects.
The executable examples show why that rule loses information: matching subjects can describe
different causes, and different subjects can describe the same cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

For this incident, look up the tenant's locale and form version, then inspect the report's channel.
Use `Intl.DateTimeFormat(...).formatToParts(...)` through the supplied `dayFirst` function rather
than maintaining a hand-written list of locales. An unknown tenant produces no classified cause;
it is not permission to merge two unknown reports together.

The desk should replace subject-only merging with a new reviewed decision based on this evidence.
Decision 0001 remains unchanged as an accepted historical record. This review recommends a change;
it does not invent approval from the desk.

## Evidence and refresh procedure

Track the fixes separately in [the parser issue](https://github.com/1760272981/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
and [the form-hint issue](https://github.com/1760272981/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).

The exported reports, tenant directory, desk notes, and supplied classification functions are the
evidence used here. Run `npm run build` after updating those inputs. The page's tables, annotations,
timeline, and desk reference are calculated by components; the issue drafts and report-ID membership
lists are regenerated by `npm run audit`. Review the changes before publishing updated issue counts.

The GitHub issues track the two separate problems identified here. Their report totals describe this
export snapshot, not a live count of the desk's queue. The rendered copy of this page is a build artifact;
rebuild it when the source or data changes.

